  "query_preview": "..Water vapor imagery shows a fairly anomalous \nupper level low centered over the Reno/Lake Tahoe area with \nridging over the northern Great Plains and Midwest. Models keep \nthis low over the same general area through Thursday, then open \nit up Friday as it moves eastward through the Great Basin. This \nwill keep temperatures 3-6 degrees below normal through Saturday\nfor most of the area. Far western Pima County will be 10-14 \ndegrees below normal through Friday thanks to surface moisture \nmoving in from the Gulf of California and its proximity to the \nlow. The low will also keep winds breezy through Friday with the\nstrongest winds expected today. This will lead to elevated fire\nweather conditions each day through Friday from central Pima \nCounty eastward. RH values will be too high in far western Pima \nCounty. Today some areas in central Pima County could see \nlocalized and brief critical fire weather conditions. Patchy \nblowing dust could also be possible over the Tohono O'Odham \nNation. \n\nAfter Saturday ensembles keep the main storm track to the north\nwith high pressure slowly starting to build in over the Four\nCorners or southern Plains. They also hint at a weak cutoff low\nforming near Baja early to mid next week. With heights\nincreasing, so will temperatures. The warmup begins Sunday with\ntemperatures 0-4 degrees above normal through the middle of the\nweek. Under the current setup this pattern could also usher in\nmoisture from the east. Lots of uncertainty still with how far \neast the moisture will end up, but right now the best moisture \nlooks to stay just east of Tucson with NBM PoPs 20-40 percent \nTuesday into Wednesday.",
